// TEST: OrbIdx Wreath Decompact Full
// FULL decompaction of a wreath class -- the endpoint of
// docs/plan-orbidx-decompaction.md §3's lattice, and the only point on it v1
// builds. `decompact(W, 0)`: for a wreath the second argument is the number of
// LEVELS TO KEEP (§4.3), not a dimension to free, so 0 means "keep none" =
// fully dense. The result is the rank-prod(ri) tensor, one plain Idx<n> axis
// per raw axis, and EVERY cell is the class's own §2 read. 218 pins that any
// other `d` (the §3 peel) is refused by name.
//
// DENSE-SEQUENTIAL, in both backends: one `orb_read` per dense cell rather than
// §4.2's pool-sequential scatter. The scatter would have to enumerate each pool
// cell's ORBIT with its character, and no verified emitter for that exists --
// `orb_visit` hands out canonical tuples only -- whereas `orb_read` IS the
// verified §2 read. Correctness is the bar here; the performance claim in §4.2
// is unmeasured either way.
//
// THE ORACLE IS INDEPENDENT OF THE PACK, per §6: the pinned dense values below
// are the LOGICAL definition W(i,j,k,l) = S(i,j) + S(k,l) with S(i,j) =
// A[i]*A[j], evaluated at every raw tuple. Nothing in the pins is read back out
// of the pool or its order, so a rank/visit-order mismatch cannot cancel
// between the two sides the way a read->write roundtrip lets it (the antisym
// storage post-mortem).
//
// n = 3, so the 21-cell pool decompacts to 3^4 = 81 dense cells -- a 3.9x
// expansion, which is the cost §1 calls the escape hatch and §5 says always
// allocates (pack and dense share no layout, so there is no in-place form).
let A = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
let g = lambda(x, y) where comm(x, y) -> x * y
let S = method_for(A, A) <@> g |> compute
let h = lambda(p, q) where comm(p, q) -> p + q
let W = method_for(S, S) <@> h |> compute
let D = decompact(W, 0)
// S(i,j) = A[i]*A[j] as a full 3x3 = [[1,2,3],[2,4,6],[3,6,9]], so the
// (i,j) plane of D is the flattened S(i,j) + [1,2,3,2,4,6,3,6,9]. D is rank 4
// and prints as ONE flat row-major run (the auto-printer's single rank
// regime; the per-plane `D[i][j] = [...]` grid lines these pins used to ride
// are retired), so the D pin is those nine planes in (i,j) order:
//   (0,0) (0,1) (0,2) (1,0) (1,1) (1,2) (2,0) (2,1) (2,2)
// EXPECT: S = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9]
// EXPECT: W = [2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 10, 4, 5, 6, 8, 11, 6, 7, 9, 12, 8, 10, 13, 12, 15, 18]
// EXPECT: D = [2, 3, 4, 3, 5, 7, 4, 7, 10, 3, 4, 5, 4, 6, 8, 5, 8, 11, 4, 5, 6, 5, 7, 9, 6, 9, 12, 3, 4, 5, 4, 6, 8, 5, 8, 11, 5, 6, 7, 6, 8, 10, 7, 10, 13, 7, 8, 9, 8, 10, 12, 9, 12, 15, 4, 5, 6, 5, 7, 9, 6, 9, 12, 7, 8, 9, 8, 10, 12, 9, 12, 15, 10, 11, 12, 11, 13, 15, 12, 15, 18]
